From: Android Security Research
Subject: [PATCH] research: log every incoming Java Binder transaction

RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION — NOT FOR PRODUCTION.

This patch adds a build-time-gated trace to android.os.Binder.execTransact,
which is the single funnel through which EVERY incoming Java Binder call passes
in EVERY process on the device (see 07-AOSP/README.md section 4.1 step 8).

Applied to a userdebug image and enabled at runtime, it produces a complete,
system-wide record of framework IPC with the kernel-stamped caller identity —
below anything a user-space application can hook, detect or disable.

WHY THIS RATHER THAN FRIDA
    Frida hooks the same method and is available anywhere, which makes it the
    right tool when you cannot build an image. But a Frida hook lives in user
    space inside the traced process, so an application with native anti-hook
    logic can find it. This patch is in the platform, in a process the
    application does not control, and it captures processes that started before
    you attached.

    Use Frida when you cannot build. Use this when you can.

HOW TO APPLY
    cd <aosp-tree>
    git -C frameworks/base apply --check ../../path/to/04-binder-trace.patch
    git -C frameworks/base apply       ../../path/to/04-binder-trace.patch

    source build/envsetup.sh
    lunch aosp_cf_x86_64_phone-trunk_staging-userdebug
    m framework
    m snod                        # repack system.img in under a minute
    stop_cvd && launch_cvd --daemon

HOW TO ENABLE AT RUNTIME
    The trace is off by default so the image is usable normally. Turn it on
    with a system property, which costs one boolean read per transaction when
    off:

    adb root
    adb shell setprop persist.research.binder_trace 1
    adb shell stop && adb shell start          # restart the framework
    adb logcat -s BINDER_TRACE

    # off again
    adb shell setprop persist.research.binder_trace 0

HOW TO ANALYSE
    adb logcat -c
    adb shell am start -n com.example.app/.MainActivity
    sleep 5
    adb logcat -d -s BINDER_TRACE > trace.txt

    # which interfaces, how often
    grep -oE 'iface=[^ ]+' trace.txt | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20

    # everything one UID asked the system for
    grep 'uid=10123' trace.txt

    # every distinct (interface, code) pair — the app's real API surface usage
    grep -oE 'iface=[^ ]+ code=[0-9]+' trace.txt | sort -u

WARNING
    A patched platform is a RESEARCH INSTRUMENT, not evidence. Reproduce every
    finding on an unmodified image before it goes in a report, and state in the
    report which image each piece of evidence came from. The failure mode is
    embarrassing and common: a "vulnerability" that exists only because
    something was changed three weeks earlier and forgotten.

    Do not ship an image with this enabled. The log contains the full IPC
    behaviour of every application on the device, including argument sizes and
    timing, and logd is readable by more than you think.

NOTE ON THIS PATCH FILE
    AOSP moves. The exact signature of execTransact and the surrounding code
    have changed across releases, so this patch is provided in a form you must
    adapt rather than apply blindly. Find the current signature with:

        cs.android.com   file:Binder.java function:execTransact

    and place the instrumentation block at the top of the method body, before
    any dispatch. The block itself is release-independent.

---
 core/java/android/os/Binder.java | 62 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 62 insertions(+)

diff --git a/core/java/android/os/Binder.java b/core/java/android/os/Binder.java
--- a/core/java/android/os/Binder.java
+++ b/core/java/android/os/Binder.java
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 package android.os;

 import android.annotation.NonNull;
+import android.util.Slog;

 /**
  * Base class for a remotable object, the core part of a lightweight
@@ -60,6 +61,45 @@ public class Binder implements IBinder {

     private static volatile String sProxyTransactListenerClassName = null;

+    // ================== RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION ==========================
+    //
+    // Every incoming Java Binder transaction in this process passes through
+    // execTransact() below. Logging here therefore captures ALL framework IPC
+    // served by this process, with the caller identity that the KERNEL stamped
+    // into the transaction in binder_transaction() — an identity the caller
+    // cannot forge.
+    //
+    // Gated on a system property so an instrumented image remains usable:
+    //     adb shell setprop persist.research.binder_trace 1
+    //     adb shell stop && adb shell start
+    //
+    // See 07-AOSP/README.md sections 3.10, 4.1 and 9.3.
+    //
+    private static final String RESEARCH_TRACE_TAG = "BINDER_TRACE";
+    private static final boolean RESEARCH_TRACE_ENABLED =
+            SystemProperties.getBoolean("persist.research.binder_trace", false);
+
+    private void researchTrace(int code, int flags) {
+        // Everything here must be exception-safe. This runs on the hot path of
+        // every IPC on the device; a throw would take down system_server.
+        try {
+            String iface = null;
+            try {
+                iface = getInterfaceDescriptor();
+            } catch (Throwable ignored) {
+                // Some Binder subclasses have no descriptor. Not an error.
+            }
+            Slog.i(RESEARCH_TRACE_TAG,
+                    "impl=" + getClass().getName()
+                    + " iface=" + (iface == null ? "(none)" : iface)
+                    + " code=" + code
+                    + " flags=" + flags
+                    + " uid=" + getCallingUid()
+                    + " pid=" + getCallingPid()
+                    + " myPid=" + Process.myPid());
+        } catch (Throwable ignored) {
+            // Never let instrumentation break the platform.
+        }
+    }
+    // ================ END RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION ========================
+
     /**
      * Return the ID of the process that sent you the current transaction
      * that is being processed.
@@ -1200,6 +1240,12 @@ public class Binder implements IBinder {
     // Entry point from android_util_Binder.cpp's onTransact.
     private boolean execTransact(int code, long dataObj, long replyObj,
             int flags) {
+        // ---- RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION ----
+        if (RESEARCH_TRACE_ENABLED) {
+            researchTrace(code, flags);
+        }
+        // ---- END RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION ----
+
         // At that point, the parcel request headers haven't been parsed so we do not know what
         // WorkSource the caller has set. Use calling UID as the default.
         final int callingUid = Binder.getCallingUid();
--
2.43.0


================================================================================
COMPANION PATCHES
================================================================================

The same technique applies at four other points. Keep each as a separate,
reviewed patch file in a git repository, so every experiment can state exactly
which instrumentation was active and a colleague can recreate your environment.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. NATIVE BINDER — frameworks/native/libs/binder/IPCThreadState.cpp
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Catches OUTBOUND transactions, including from native processes that have no Java
at all (surfaceflinger, mediaserver, vendor daemons). The Java patch above sees
only inbound Java calls; this one completes the picture.

    status_t IPCThreadState::transact(int32_t handle, uint32_t code,
                                      const Parcel& data, Parcel* reply,
                                      uint32_t flags)
    {
    +   // ---- RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION ----
    +   if (__android_log_is_loggable(ANDROID_LOG_INFO, "BINDER_NATIVE", ANDROID_LOG_INFO)) {
    +       ALOGI("BINDER_NATIVE pid=%d uid=%d handle=%d code=%u size=%zu flags=%u",
    +             getpid(), getuid(), handle, code, data.dataSize(), flags);
    +   }
    +   // ---- END RESEARCH INSTRUMENTATION ----
        // ... existing implementation

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. PERMISSION DECISIONS
   frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/permission/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The class and method names have moved between releases. Find the current one:

    cs.android.com  path:services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/permission function:checkPermission

Then log the final decision:

    +   Slog.i("PERM_TRACE", "perm=" + permName
    +           + " pkg=" + packageName
    +           + " uid=" + Binder.getCallingUid()
    +           + " pid=" + Binder.getCallingPid()
    +           + " user=" + userId
    +           + " result=" + (result == PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED
    +                              ? "GRANTED" : "DENIED"));

This turns "which permission actually blocked this?" from a black-box question
into a log line, and it is the fastest way to understand an unfamiliar OEM's
permission customisations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. LIBRARY LOADS — bionic/linker/linker.cpp
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every dlopen on the device, including from native code that deliberately avoids
System.loadLibrary. Useful for packers, protectors and injected libraries.

    +   async_safe_format_log(ANDROID_LOG_INFO, "DLOPEN_TRACE",
    +           "pid=%d name=%s flags=0x%x", getpid(), name, flags);

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. KEYSTORE OPERATIONS — system/security/keystore2/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every key creation, use and deletion, with the calling UID and the key's
purposes and authorisations. Answers "is this key really hardware-backed and
really user-authentication-bound?" without trusting the app's own claims.
See 49-KeyStore/README.md.

================================================================================
MANAGING A PATCH SERIES
================================================================================

    ~/aosp-research-patches/
      README.md                          what each patch does and why
      frameworks_base.binder-trace.patch
      frameworks_base.perm-trace.patch
      frameworks_native.binder-native.patch
      bionic.dlopen-trace.patch
      system_security.keystore-trace.patch

Apply them with the loop in ../05-research-env/setup.sh, which names each patch
file after the project it applies to. Keeping them in git means:

  - every experiment records exactly which instrumentation was active;
  - you can rebuild a CLEAN image on demand to reproduce a finding;
  - a colleague can recreate your environment exactly;
  - a patch that stops applying after a release bump is a visible, reviewable
    event rather than a silent drift.
